  <static seed="25">
    <statement><p>A density function is given by <m>f(x)=\frac{1}{5}e^{-x/5}</m> for <m>x\geq 0\text{.}</m> The mode is defined as the <m>x</m> value that maximizes <m>f(x)\text{.}</m> Find the mode of <m>f(x)\text{.}</m></p>
<p>Mode: <fillin characters="10" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>0</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>Since</p>
<p><me>\begin{aligned}
                    f'(x)
                    \amp = -\frac{1}{5^2}e^{-x/5},
                    \end{aligned}</me></p>
<p>the derivative is always negative for <m>x\geq 0\text{.}</m> Therefore, the density is decreasing on its domain, so its maximum occurs at the left endpoint. The mode is <m>x=0\text{.}</m></p>
</solution>
